The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (READ_ONCE(msk->snd_data_fin_enable) && !(ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN)) {
    tcp_shutdown(ssk, SEND_SHUTDOWN);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because if the MPTCP socket is shut down before the fallback to TCP is initiated, the condition checks for `snd_data_fin_enable` and whether the socket has already been shut down (`ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN`) may not account for the scenario where all pending data has been spooled. As a result, the TCP connection may not be closed properly, leading to resource leaks.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (READ_ONCE(msk->snd_data_fin_enable) && !(ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN)) {
    ssk->sk_shutdown |= SEND_SHUTDOWN;
    tcp_shutdown(ssk, SEND_SHUTDOWN);
}
```
Reason:
The fix explicitly checks the critical condition at the time of fallback to ensure that if the MPTCP socket is in a state where it should be shutting down, it sets the `ssk->sk_shutdown` flag before calling `tcp_shutdown`. This ensures that the TCP connection is properly closed, preventing the resource leak and undefined behavior that occurs when the socket is shut down prematurely.

